{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "PlumeRise",
    "description": "PlumeRise is a self-contained, one-dimensional atmospheric plume model with 200 levels on staggered scalar and vertical-velocity grids. Prescribed atmospheric profiles and surface fire heat and moisture fluxes drive the evolution of plume temperature, vertical and horizontal velocity, radius, water vapor, cloud water, rainwater, ice and a passive tracer. The formulation includes buoyancy and condensate loading, entrainment, ambient wind exchange, vertical advection, turbulent diffusion, sedimentation, Rayleigh damping and latent heating from microphysical phase changes.\n\nParenthesized superscripts identify microphysical stages, not mathematical exponents. For temperature and water-species states, (0) denotes the state before phase changes; (1) follows cloud condensation or evaporation; (2) rain evaporation; (3) ice sublimation; (4) vapor deposition onto ice; (5) glaciation by rain freezing; and (6) ice melting. Each state includes the preceding applicable processes. Warm-rain autoconversion and accretion follow these stages. The interior transfer interval is τμ = τₛ/3; lower-boundary microphysics uses 3τμ = τₛ.\n\nThe default native tests compare independently calculated numerical results with the Freitas-derived Fortran implementation, including Mallia/Wilmot modifications, for the prescribed atmosphere and forcing over 0–300 s. Minute-indexed forcing (the heat-flux ramp and the velocity-filter weight) is evaluated at the end of the current update, t + τₙ, as in the reference finite-step scheme; with this convention all 57 comparisons satisfy the established tolerances, although plume-head values remain below the reference at 300 s (vertical velocity at levels 6–11 by about 7–11%, tracer at levels 7–11 by about 4–8%). These comparisons provide limited early-time screening; they do not establish full-duration agreement or validate significant precipitation or glaciation. The complete extended scientific validation report is supplied separately.\n\nWhy the model carries water and ice: the fire and the entrained air supply water vapor, which can condense as the plume rises and cools. Condensation, deposition and freezing release latent heat that warms the plume and adds buoyancy; evaporation, sublimation and melting absorb heat; and cloud water, rain and ice add weight that reduces the buoyancy ℬₜ. These processes control the rise of large fire plumes that form pyrocumulus clouds, so the staged microphysics is part of the plume dynamics. In the default 0–300 s test the plume stays almost unsaturated, so these terms are present but nearly inactive.\n\nConventions: physical constants and fixed coefficients are parameters with constant values, not solved quantities. All mixing ratios q are mass mixing ratios (kg of water per kg of dry air); units of every variable are given in its units field.\n\nNotation: A denotes vertical-advection tendencies, 𝒱 eddy-diffusion tendencies, E ambient exchange, M microphysical tendencies, and 𝒮 sedimentation. Subscripts v, l, r and i denote vapor, cloud liquid, rain and ice. The subscript 0 denotes the lower boundary; k is a one-based vertical level. In a conditional expression ifelse(condition, a, b), use a when the condition holds and b otherwise. The spatial derivative operators use the stated staggered-grid differences and boundary rules."
  },
  "solver": {
    "abstol": 1e-10,
    "reltol": 1e-09,
    "stiffness": "high"
  },
  "domain": {
    "independent_variable": "t",
    "element_type": "Float64"
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NZ": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 200,
      "description": "Number of vertical levels in each staggered grid, including prescribed boundary levels."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "zt": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NZ"
    },
    "zm": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NZ"
    }
  },
